# JavaScript Code Splitting Strategies

This document covers code splitting patterns using webpack's dynamic imports to efficiently load JavaScript code, ensuring smaller initial bundles and faster load times.

## Overview

Code splitting allows you to:
- Load JavaScript only when needed
- Reduce initial bundle size
- Improve page load performance
- Load different code for different devices

## Conditional Loading

Load code only when needed based on DOM elements:

### Basic Pattern

```javascript
initHoverIntent() {
    // Check if feature is supported
    if (this.supportsHover) {
        // Check if elements exist
        const $hoverIntentElements = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('.hover-intent'))

        if ($hoverIntentElements && $hoverIntentElements.length > 0) {
            // Dynamically import only when needed
            import(/* webpackChunkName: "hover-intent" */ "./functions/hover-intent")
                .then(({default: initHoverIntent}) => {
                    initHoverIntent($hoverIntentElements)
                })
                .catch((error) => console.log("Error importing hover-intent:", error))
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Points

1. **Check feature support first** - `if (this.supportsHover)`
2. **Check if elements exist** - `if ($hoverIntentElements.length > 0)`
3. **Use webpack magic comment** - `/* webpackChunkName: "hover-intent" */`
4. **Handle errors** - `.catch()` for import failures

## Device-Specific Loading

Load different code for mobile vs desktop devices:

```javascript
class Frontend {
    constructor() {
        // Get window width once at initialization
        this.windowWidth = window.innerWidth ||
                          document.documentElement.clientWidth ||
                          document.body.clientWidth

        this.mobileBreakPoint = 1023

        // Load appropriate navigation based on device
        this.initNavigation()
    }

    initNavigation() {
        // Desktop navigation
        if (this.windowWidth > this.mobileBreakPoint) {
            this.initMainNavigation()
        }
        // Mobile navigation
        else {
            this.initSideNavigation()
        }
    }

    initMainNavigation() {
        const $mainNavigationElement = document.querySelector('.main-navigation')

        if ($mainNavigationElement) {
            import(/* webpackChunkName: "main-navigation" */ "./classes/MainNavigation")
                .then(({default: MainNavigation}) => {
                    new MainNavigation($mainNavigationElement)
                })
                .catch((error) => console.log("Error importing MainNavigation:", error))
        }
    }

    initSideNavigation() {
        const $sideNavigationElement = document.querySelector('.side-navigation')

        if ($sideNavigationElement) {
            import(/* webpackChunkName: "side-navigation" */ "./classes/SideNavigation")
                .then(({default: SideNavigation}) => {
                    new SideNavigation($sideNavigationElement)
                })
                .catch((error) => console.log("Error importing SideNavigation:", error))
        }
    }
}
```

### Benefits

- Mobile users don't download desktop navigation code
- Desktop users don't download mobile navigation code
- Reduces JavaScript payload by ~50% for navigation code

## Per-Section Loading

Sections load their own JavaScript only when they are present on the page. `SectionReferenceTrait` handles this — pass `$hasScript = true` to `registerReferenceSection()` when `sections/[section-slug]/js/[section-slug].js` exists.

### PHP section class

```php
<?php

namespace TechnoSapiens;

use TechnoSapiens\Core\Singleton;

class SectionExample extends Singleton {
    use SectionReferenceTrait;

    public const SECTION_ID = 'section_section_example';
    public const SECTION_SLUG = 'section-example';

    protected function __construct() {
        $this->registerReferenceSection(
            __('Example section.', Theme::TEXT_DOMAIN),
            'admin-generic',
            true,
            [],
            []
        );
    }

    public static function getSectionLabel(): string {
        return __('Example', Theme::TEXT_DOMAIN);
    }
}
```

The trait enqueues `section-example-script.js` and `section-example-style.css` from the webpack manifest when `has_block('acf/section-section-example')` is true.

### Key features

1. **Global post check** — checks `$post` and `$otherPosts`
2. **Conditional loading** — only when the section is on the page
3. **Dependency management** — JS depends on `Theme::TEXT_DOMAIN . '_scripts'`
4. **Prefer no JS** — use native HTML/CSS first (see `section-accordion` with `<details>`)

## Webpack Magic Comments

Use webpack magic comments to name chunks for better debugging:

```javascript
// Named chunk for better debugging
import(/* webpackChunkName: "material-input" */ "./classes/MaterialInput")

// Multiple magic comments
import(
    /* webpackChunkName: "accordion" */
    /* webpackMode: "lazy" */
    "./classes/Accordion"
)
```

### Generated Chunk Names

This generates recognizable chunk names in your build:

```
technosapiens-chunk-material-input.min.js
technosapiens-chunk-main-navigation.min.js
technosapiens-chunk-hover-intent.min.js
technosapiens-chunk-side-navigation.min.js
technosapiens-chunk-section-example.min.js
```

### Benefits

1. **Easier Debugging** - Clear names in network tab
2. **Better Cache Control** - Named chunks are cached better
3. **Code Organization** - Easy to see what chunks are loading

## Static Methods for Shared Functionality

Use static methods for functionality not tied to a specific instance:

```javascript
class Frontend {
    constructor() {
        // Get device information
        this.windowWidth = window.innerWidth ||
                          document.documentElement.clientWidth ||
                          document.body.clientWidth

        this.supportsHover = window.matchMedia('(hover: hover)').matches &&
                            !('ontouchstart' in window)

        // Instance methods - device-specific
        this.initHoverIntent()
        this.initMainNavigation()

        // Static methods - always available
        Frontend.initGravityForms()
        Frontend.initMaterialInputs()
    }

    // Instance method - checks device capabilities
    initHoverIntent() {
        if (this.supportsHover) {
            const $hoverIntentElements = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('.hover-intent'))
            if ($hoverIntentElements.length > 0) {
                import(/* webpackChunkName: "hover-intent" */ "./functions/hover-intent")
                    .then(({default: initHoverIntent}) => {
                        initHoverIntent($hoverIntentElements)
                    })
            }
        }
    }

    // Static method - not tied to instance
    static initMaterialInputs() {
        const selectors = [...Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('.material-input'))]

        if (selectors.length > 0) {
            import(/* webpackChunkName: "material-input" */ "./classes/MaterialInput")
                .then(({default: MaterialInput}) => {
                    selectors.forEach(($containerElement) => {
                        const $labelElement = $containerElement.querySelector('label')
                        const $inputElement = $containerElement.querySelector('input, textarea')
                        new MaterialInput($containerElement, $labelElement, $inputElement)
                    })
                })
                .catch((error) => console.log("Error importing MaterialInput:", error))
        }
    }

    // Static method - jQuery compatibility
    static initGravityForms() {
        window.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
            if (typeof jQuery !== 'undefined') {
                // Re-init material inputs when Gravity Forms renders
                jQuery(document).on('gform_post_render', () => {
                    Frontend.initMaterialInputs()
                })
            }
        })
    }
}

// Initialize
new Frontend()
```

### When to Use Static Methods

✅ **Use static methods when:**
- Functionality doesn't depend on instance state
- Method needs to be called from external code
- Re-initializing components (like after AJAX)
- Utility functions

❌ **Don't use static methods when:**
- Need access to `this.state`
- Require device-specific checks (windowWidth, supportsHover)
- Managing component lifecycle

## Code Splitting Checklist

Consider code splitting when:

- [ ] The code is larger than 10KB minified
- [ ] The code is not used on every page
- [ ] The code is only needed for specific user interactions
- [ ] The code is device-specific (mobile/desktop)
- [ ] The code is block-specific
- [ ] The code depends on user preferences or permissions

### Example: When to Split

✅ **Good candidates for splitting:**
```javascript
// Gallery - only needed on pages with galleries
initGallery()

// Video player - only needed on pages with videos
initVideoPlayer()

// Map - only needed on pages with maps
initMapbox()

// Mobile menu - only needed on mobile devices
initSideNavigation()

// Hover effects - only needed on hover-capable devices
initHoverIntent()
```

❌ **Not good for splitting:**
```javascript
// Small utilities (< 2KB)
formatCurrency()

// Used on every page
initCookieNotice()

// Critical for first render
initHeaderScroll()
```

## Performance Best Practices

### 1. Minimize Bundle Size

```javascript
// ✅ Good - Only import what you need
import(/* webpackChunkName: "accordion" */ "./classes/Accordion")
    .then(({default: Accordion}) => new Accordion($element))

// ❌ Bad - Importing entire library
import(/* webpackChunkName: "lodash" */ "lodash")
    .then((_) => _.debounce(fn, 300))
```

### 2. Avoid Import Waterfalls

```javascript
// ❌ Bad - Sequential loading (slow)
async function init() {
    const {default: ModuleA} = await import('./ModuleA')
    const {default: ModuleB} = await import('./ModuleB')  // Waits for ModuleA
    const {default: ModuleC} = await import('./ModuleC')  // Waits for ModuleB
}

// ✅ Good - Parallel loading (fast)
async function init() {
    const [
        {default: ModuleA},
        {default: ModuleB},
        {default: ModuleC}
    ] = await Promise.all([
        import('./ModuleA'),
        import('./ModuleB'),
        import('./ModuleC')
    ])
}
```

### 3. Cache Chunks Appropriately

Webpack automatically handles chunk caching through content hashing. Just ensure:

1. Use magic comments for consistent naming
2. Don't change chunk names unnecessarily
3. Let webpack handle cache busting

### 4. Monitor Bundle Sizes

```bash
# Analyze webpack bundle
npm run build -- --analyze

# Check chunk sizes
ls -lh dist/js/
```

## Common Patterns

### Pattern: Feature Detection + Conditional Load

```javascript
initFeature() {
    // 1. Check feature support
    if (!this.supportsFeature) return

    // 2. Check if elements exist
    const $elements = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('.feature'))
    if (!$elements.length) return

    // 3. Load code
    import(/* webpackChunkName: "feature" */ "./functions/feature")
        .then(({default: initFeature}) => initFeature($elements))
        .catch((error) => console.log("Error loading feature:", error))
}
```

### Pattern: Device-Specific Component

```javascript
initComponent() {
    const $element = document.querySelector('.component')
    if (!$element) return

    // Load different component based on device
    const chunkName = this.windowWidth > 1023 ? 'component-desktop' : 'component-mobile'

    import(/* webpackChunkName: "[request]" */ `./components/${chunkName}`)
        .then(({default: Component}) => new Component($element))
        .catch((error) => console.log(`Error loading ${chunkName}:`, error))
}
```

### Pattern: User Interaction Triggered Load

```javascript
// Only load when user clicks
$button.addEventListener('click', async () => {
    const {default: Modal} = await import(/* webpackChunkName: "modal" */ "./classes/Modal")
    const modal = new Modal({ title: 'Example' })
    modal.open()
}, { once: true }) // Only load once
```

